{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792310636,"nonce":"bKQAhPgkQLw37Cg5Dj85OuV8O5w=","title":"InternedString had a Display that printed `InternedString(4469)`, so any {} or .to_string() on a name produced a name-shaped string that ...","message":"FOUND + FIXED 2026-08-15. `compiler/src/tast/string_intern.rs` carried:\n\n    impl std::fmt::Display for InternedString {\n        fn fmt(\u0026self, f) -\u003e Result { write!(f, \"InternedString({})\", self.0) }\n    }\n\n`InternedString(u32)` is an index into a PER-PROCESS interner, so `{}` or\n`.to_string()` on any name produced the literal text `InternedString(4469)` —\nname-shaped, matching nothing, and DIFFERENT BETWEEN RUNS because interner ids\nvary. 34 call sites did exactly that.\n\nHOW IT WAS FOUND: byte-diffing two `.blade` artifacts for a determinism oracle\nturned up a len-20 string inside Array.map/Array.filter bodies. Removing the\nDisplay impl turns every offending call site into a compile error — **the error\nlist IS the census**. Cheap, exhaustive technique worth reusing for any\n\"which callers stringify X\" question.\n\nREAL DAMAGE (all on the live path):\n- `ir/mir/expr/functions.rs:133` — every function PARAMETER's `IrLocal.name`\n  was `\"InternedString(N)\"`. Garbage debug names, serialized into artifacts,\n  and program-dependent (blocks reproducible artifacts).\n- `ir/mir/helpers/variables.rs:232/241` — `attr.name.to_string()` compared\n  against `\"inline_candidate\"` / `\"straight_line_code\"` etc. Could NEVER match,\n  so the whole SSA-optimization-hint pipeline was DEAD.\n- `ir/mir/stmt/switch.rs:580` — a field offset computed as `name.len()`, so it\n  was ~19-21 depending on the interner id's DIGIT COUNT. (Already an\n  acknowledged TODO placeholder; now at least deterministic.)\n- `ir/mir/expr/dispatch.rs:379` (string-pool const path) and\n  `expr/literals.rs:805` (interpolation literal parts) — would emit\n  `\"InternedString(N)\"` AS THE STRING VALUE. Latent: a test of interpolation and\n  default-arg literals printed correctly, so these paths are not hit commonly.\n- ~26 diagnostic sites printed ids instead of names in user-facing errors.\n\n- `compiler/examples/test_abstract_types.rs:223` — `m.name.to_string().contains(\"add\")`\n  as an ASSERTION INPUT. Never true, so `has_op_method` was always false and the\n  operator-overloading check tested nothing. **Run the census with\n  `--all-targets --keep-going`**: a plain `cargo check -p compiler` misses\n  examples and tests entirely, and three example files had live sites.\n\nFIX: Display REMOVED (it is a footgun — an id type must not render as text).\n7 MIR sites resolve through the interner via a new\n`HirToMirContext::interned_str` helper; the rest use `{:?}`, which prints the\nsame text Display did, so messages are unchanged but can no longer masquerade\nas names.\n\n**THE SSA-HINT FIX IS INERT IN PRODUCTION — verify before claiming a perf\nchange.** The producer `tast_to_hir.rs::extract_ssa_optimization_hints` is gated\non `if let Some(semantic_graphs) = self.semantic_graphs` (:918), and\n`compilation.rs` NEVER sets it (`tast_to_hir.rs:136` = `None`); see\n[[reference_pipeline_ground_truth_vs_docs]] — semantic_graph is not built on the\nCLI path.\n\n**LANDMINE LEFT IN PLACE:** if semantic analysis is ever enabled, the hint\n`straight_line_code` (emitted merely when `phi_count == 0`, i.e. no branches)\nsets `func.attributes.pure = true` — \"Assume pure for straight-line code\"\n(`ir/mir/expr/functions.rs:101`). Straight-line is NOT pure: `function setX(v)\n{ this.x = v; }` qualifies. That would let the optimizer drop or CSE calls with\nside effects. It has been double-dead (no semantic graphs AND the broken string\ncompare). FIX THE PURITY INFERENCE BEFORE ENABLING ANALYSIS.\n\nRelated: [[feedback_no_silent_dispatch_fallthrough]] — a comparison that can\nnever match is the same disease as a silent fallback.\n\n---\nArchived from Claude memory `bugs_internedstring_display_forged_names` on 2026-10-18. A record of past work; open items in it may have their own issues.","files":null}]}